  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "db0a22a6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "** Interpretation of the confusion matrix:**\n",
    "- **True Positives (~430):** customers correctly flagged as likely to churn — the retention team can proactively reach out to them.\n",
    "- **False Negatives (~131):** actual churners the model misses.\n",
    "- **False Positives (~420):** loyal customers incorrectly flagged\n",
    "- **True Negatives (~1132):** correctly identified loyal customers.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Precision vs. Recall — which matters more here?\n",
    "\n",
    "**For this telecom churn use case, we prioritize Recall over Precision.**\n",
    "\n",
    "The reasoning: a **false negative** (a real churner the model misses) means the company loses that customer's entire future revenue with zero chance to intervene. A **false positive** (a loyal customer incorrectly flagged) simply costs the company a modest, often low-cost retention offer — a much cheaper mistake. Since the downside of missing a churner is larger than the downside of a wasted retention offer, we accept lower precision in exchange for higher recall. This is exactly why `class_weight='balanced'` was used for the final model — it trades some precision for substantially better recall (0.77 vs. 0.60 in the baseline).\n",
    "\n",
    "Precision shouldn't be ignored entirely — if retention offers were very expensive, the business would want to move the probability threshold to balance the two costs, which the API's `churn_probability` output enables.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
